Independent blind design comparison. Read-only; no implementation, delegation, prior scores, other reviews or source. Compare baseline and A/B/C for unavailable omen, question composer and offline Circles. Return twelve scene/variant rows aggregating provided states/scales. Score six dimensions; null where prototype behavior cannot be established. Choose strongest coherent variant per scene; baseline may win. No desired score.
FROZEN RUBRIC:
## Rubric: score the screen AND its components

Grade each dimension 0–10. Aesthetic score A = mean of hierarchy, typography/spacing, surface/color coherence. Functional score F = mean of task clarity, feedback/recovery, accessibility. Overall = 0.4A + 0.6F, rounded to one decimal. Apply the same six dimensions to each screen and each meaningful component (orb, tabs, option, input, card, button, modal). Mark genuinely inapplicable dimensions N/A and normalize; never infer an interaction score from a screenshot.

- **6 — usable but visibly rough:** task succeeds; inconsistent spacing, competing emphasis, or weak feedback is obvious. No data loss or unreachable primary action.
- **8 — clean and dependable:** one clear next action; consistent type/spacing/surface roles; responsive press/loading/error states; 44pt targets; text enlargement, contrast and reduced motion checked. Minor polish remains.
- **9 — premium and effortless:** all 8 criteria plus continuity through transitions, contextual recovery, concise humane copy, restrained delight, no distracting layout shifts; real phone and assistive-technology checks passed.
- **10 — exemplary for this task:** all 9 criteria; repeated novice use and representative accessibility/performance checks find no material friction. Reserve for demonstrated outcomes, not taste or resemblance to another app.
- **0–5:** 0 absent/unusable; 2 major path broken; 4 task possible only with workarounds; 5 works but a serious clarity/accessibility issue remains.

Hard gates: blocked or materially unanswered task/data loss/error disguised as success => overall at most 4. Essential unreadable content, inaccessible primary control or motion preference ignored => at most 5. Unverified runtime/native behavior => provisional grade, cannot certify 9/10. Averages never erase a failed gate.

Loop acceptance: every core screen and critical component >=8, A and F each >=8, no hard-gate failure. Record before/after screenshot at 390×844, action and result, normal/200% text, reduced motion, empty/loading/error/selected state and any untested boundary. Change one issue at a time; keep every failed observation. Browser checks do not prove native haptics, voice, keyboard or screen reader behavior.



Authorized loop threshold is A >=9 and F >=9 for every screen and meaningful component from both independent graders. This supersedes the report's >=8 acceptance sentence; its evidence requirements and hard gates remain unchanged. Native and physical assistive-technology coverage is unverified, so no 9/10 certification is permitted on that evidence. Grade honestly; the target is not a requested score.


Stage clarification from firstmate inbox004: continue scoring the browser implementation provisionally until every screen and component reaches A>=9 and F>=9 there; firstmate will then arrange the physical iPhone/VoiceOver pass. For this stage, judge the demonstrated browser behavior across the full numeric range and record missing physical/native proof separately, rather than lowering every browser score solely for that deferred boundary. This does NOT certify a full/native9/10 or waive a browser accessibility defect. Keep all other anchors, dimensions and failure gates unchanged. Never award a target score merely to complete the loop.

Baseline is actual current production.127tests,282layout states across360/390/430 normal200%,1662targets,489contrast checks,16entrances,actual local-model and60-second recovery flows pass. Seven opening modes pass and isolated normal/golden/ambient timing near60fps,max16.8ms. Fresh Chrome/WebKit Home passes. Baseline explicit entry, question retention and provider grant/withdrawal/separate Ask verified; native/physical proof deferred. These supplied facts apply to baseline, not prototype behavior.
All captures390px wide,844px tall, plus500px composer; normal200% text. Fonts are selected static Fraunces/Manrope with protected wordmark. Open mist is fixed. Alternatives are throwaway HTML; no model, storage, permission or social operation occurs. Controls illustrate intended routes. Do not credit unimplemented focus, draft, network, privacy enforcement, native behavior or actual keyboard. Prototype checks establish fonts, page errors and horizontal document overflow only. Screenshots include initial and decision scroll positions and B expanded context.
OMEN: baseline says No omen available / Today's attempt is used / Next attempt tomorrow. A offers continuous prose explaining one daily attempt, even when no omen is saved. B keeps tomorrow visible and exposes policy through Why no omen. C puts daily status in a persistent band between hero content and navigation. Every option preserves one durable attempt per local day, including failed generation or deliberately forgotten content. No screenshot establishes the particular reason for missing content; no proposed retry bypass or new successful omen. Judge complete Home hierarchy and clarity at200%, including any space taken by explanation.
COMPOSER: same question draft throughout. Baseline has fixed close/balance header, subject row, sending disclosure, question and Ask. A scrolls header, subject, disclosure, field and action as one continuous document. B uses a compact Back header, an explicit About myself / sending details disclosure, then field and action. C uses compact header, subject and field above a persistent decision tray containing sending scope and Ask. Separate provider consent remains required where configured; no alternative implies tapping a disclosure grants it. Evaluate whether the short500px enlarged viewport remains coherent, context and controls reachable and tradeoffs understandable; it is a constrained viewport, not physical keyboard proof.
CIRCLES: baseline honestly shows offline state with retry and disabled setup/eligibility controls. A places recovery first in a continuous page: explanation, prominent Retry, then privacy/setup context. B focuses on offline message and Retry, with About circles disclosure. C keeps an informational overview above persistent offline-status/Retry tray. All defer creation until connectivity returns; no successful auth/social claim. Retry in prototype only changes illustrative text. Privacy content explains sharing signs/broad themes while questions/private answers stay private.
Read only attached images and docs/ux-loop/expansion-37/checks.json if needed. Return concrete findings and honest limitations. Choose layout on demonstrated design merit, not assumed production functionality.

